    "q-140553": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140553",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following equations defines two intersecting lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?",
      "worked_solution": "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines two intersecting lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define two parallel lines:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=3$ defines two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{3}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1$ is the equation of an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
