    "q-95870": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95870",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following equations defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis,  in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?",
      "worked_solution": "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{3}\\,:\\,3{x}_{2}^{2}={x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a parabola:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}=4$ represents two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane: ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse).",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
