    "q-140554": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140554",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n\nI. ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$\n\nIII. ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$ defines a non-empty set. It represents the hyperbola ${x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
