    "q-96091": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96091",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a circle of radius $1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
