    "q-95396": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95396",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point.",
