    "q-95400": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95400",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})-2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2}+25)-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2}-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})^{2}}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis defines an empty set, also called an imaginary ellipse. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
