    "q-95399": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95399",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $10{y}_{1}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})-6\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}-20{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}+1)-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{z}_{1}={y}_{1}-1,\n$$\n\nwe get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the pair of parallel lines  ${z}_{1}=1$ and ${z}_{1}=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
