    "q-95391": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95391",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})+3\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+10{y}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1) & =8+1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
