    "q-95392": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95392",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
